  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Some lingering showers today before turning \ncooler with less humidity on Monday and Tuesday.\n\nWhile much of the remainder of the day will be dry, a pre-\nfrontal trough may help induce a few additional showers or\nthunderstorms, but these should be rather disorganized and\nisolated in nature. By the afternoon, expect more sun than \nclouds which will help crank temperatures back well into the \n80s. This is aided by a weak downslope component to the winds as\nthey shift to northwesterly. Despite the wind shift, humidity \nlevels do remain elevated with dew points in the upper 60s to \nlow 70s.\n\nHeading into tonight, an approaching cold front from the Great\nLakes may induce additional showers along and west of the\nAllegheny Front. Otherwise, this is largely a drier frontal\npassage which will also lead to cool temperatures and lower\nhumidity to start off the work week. Gradients are rather tight\nfor mid-September as a broad 1024-mb surface high approaches\nfrom the Great Lakes. This will favor a breezy northwesterly\nwind for Monday, with afternoon gusts up to 20 to 25 mph at\ntimes. Forecast highs are in the mid 70s to low 80s (5 to 15\ndegrees cooler for mountain locales). As the seasonably strong\ndome of high pressure settles over the northeastern U.S. on\nMonday night, low temperatures plunge into the upper 40s to 50s.\nThe autumnal feel to the air lingers into Tuesday before an\neventual warm up by mid-week. Plenty of sunshine can be expected\neach day, but with decreasing winds on Tuesday with the ridge\nalong the Eastern Seaboard. This all comes with dew points in\nthe upper 40s to mid 50s which is certainly a change from recent\ndays.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Warm and dry conditions through the end of the\nweek; showers possible along a cold front next weekend.\n\nA strong upper ridge builds over the region during the middle \nportion of the week supporting very warm and dry conditions. Not\nlooking at any record highs since records are in the mid 90s. \nThe ridge begins to flatten at the end of the week as it starts \nfeeling pressure from a deepening trough over Hudson Bay. A \nfront eventually sinks southward at the end of the week possibly\nbringing few-sct showers. Overall, very warm and dry this week,\nturning cooler and potentially wetter for late September.",
